WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 177: Harry S. Truman: The Manhattan Project, Potsdam, and Hiroshima (1945)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.280
[Rebecca] Today, we step into one of the most dramatic moments of the modern presidency.

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:23.040
[Rebecca] In April 1945, a modest Midwesterner who had never attended college was suddenly handed the reins of the world's most powerful nation and the secret of the ultimate weapon of destruction.

5
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Harry S.

6
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:32.800
[Rebecca] Truman, the revelation of the Manhattan Project, and the dawn of the atomic age.

7
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:38.880
[George] On Thursday afternoon, April 12, 1945, Vice President Harry S.

8
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.880
[George] Truman presided over a sleepy debate in the United States Senate.

9
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:54.800
[George] After adjourning the session, he walked to the Capitol Office of House Speaker Sam Rayburn for a late afternoon bourbon and political gossip, a ritual affectionately known as the Board of Education.

10
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.360
[George] Just as he poured a glass, Rayburn handed him a telephone receiver.

11
00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:06.880
[George] White House press secretary Steve Early urged him to come to the executive mansion immediately and quietly by the front entrance.

12
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:13.840
[George] Arriving at the White House at 5:25, Truman was ushered upstairs to Alina Roosevelt's private study.

13
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:17.040
[George] Alina placed a gentle hand upon his shoulder.

14
00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.280
[George] Harry, the president is dead.

15
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:23.440
[George] A stunned Truman stammered, is there anything I can do for you?

16
00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:29.200
[George] Alina looked into his eyes and replied with haunting clarity, is there anything we can do for you?

17
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.520
[George] For you are the one in trouble now.

18
00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:39.360
[George] At 7.09 that evening, Truman took the oath of office in the cabinet room, his hand trembling on a small Gideon Bible.

19
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:47.280
[George] The following morning, surrounded by reporters in the Capitol corridor, Truman uttered words that captured his profound humility.

20
00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:50.400
[George] Boys, if you ever pray, pray for me now.

21
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.080
[George] I don't know if you fellows ever had a load of hay fall on you.

22
00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:01.480
[George] But when they told me yesterday what had happened, I felt like the moon, the stars, and all the planets had fallen on me.

23
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:04.440
[George] In that quiet study on Pennsylvania Avenue.

24
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:12.600
[George] A humble Midwesterner who had never aspired to supreme power, found himself entrusted with the destiny of the entire free world.

25
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.320
[George] Harry S.

26
00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.560
[George] Truman was unlike any president who had preceded him in the 20th century.

27
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:25.160
[George] Born in Lamar, Missouri, in 1884, he was the last American president who did not possess a college degree.

28
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:33.080
[George] He grew up on a farm near independence, read voraciously in world history, and became an accomplished classical pianist.

29
00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:46.200
[George] During World War I, despite poor eyesight that required thick spectacles, 33-year-old Truman volunteered for combat duty in France, commanding Battery D of the 129th Field Artillery.

30
00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:57.160
[George] In the hellish mud of the Musargon Offensive, Captain Truman won the lifelong devotion of his unruly Irish Catholic troops by standing firm under German artillery barrages.

31
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:05.960
[George] Returning home, he married his childhood sweetheart, Bess Wallace, and opened a men's clothing haberdashery in downtown Kansas City.

32
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:18.840
[George] When the post-war recession forced the store into bankruptcy in 1922, Truman refused to declare personal bankruptcy, laboring for fifteen agonizing years to repay every single creditor in full.

33
00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:31.400
[George] Entering local politics under the sponsorship of Kansas City Democratic boss Tom Pendergast, Truman served as an honest, incorruptible county judge, building modern highways and courthouses.

34
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:48.720
[George] Elected to the Senate in 1934, he won national acclaim during World War II as chairman of the Truman Committee, fearlessly investigating defense waste and corporate profiteering, saving $15 billion and thousands of American lives without a single dissenting report.

35
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:59.600
[George] Truman's reputation for unyielding personal honesty and fiscal integrity during his Senate investigations made him the one man in Washington whom both parties trusted implicitly.

36
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.560
[George] Following his swearing in on the evening of April 12, Secretary of War Henry L.

37
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.200
[George] Stimson pulled the new president aside.

38
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:15.760
[George] Stimson whispered that the military was developing a new explosive of almost unbelievable destructive power, but offered no further details.

39
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.600
[George] It was not until twelve days later, on April 25, that Stimson and General Leslie R.

40
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:28.880
[George] Groves, military director of the Manhattan Project, arrived secretly at the White House with a comprehensive memorandum.

41
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:49.360
[George] Truman was stunned to learn that for three years, hidden beneath the codename Manhattan District, the United States had spent $2 billion operating secret cities at Oak Ridge, Tennessee, Hanford, Washington, and Los Alamos, New Mexico, to build the world's first atomic bomb based on nuclear fission.

42
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:58.560
[George] 130,000 scientists, engineers and workers were racing against the terrifying possibility that Nazi Germany might build the weapon first.

43
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:11.280
[George] As Stimson bluntly informed Truman, within four months, we shall in all probability have completed the most terrible weapon ever known in human history, one bomb of which could destroy a whole city.

44
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.280
[George] Truman realized that the ultimate moral burden of human history had landed squarely upon his shoulders.

45
00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:28.320
[George] The sheer magnitude of the secret Manhattan Project revealed the astonishing industrial and scientific capacity of a mobilized democracy operating under total wartime discipline.

46
00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:43.720
[George] In July 1945, Truman traveled across the Atlantic aboard the cruiser USS Augusta to Potsdam, outside the ruined ashes of Berlin, for his first and only face-to-face summit with Winston Churchill and Soviet Premier Joseph Stalin.

47
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:55.240
[George] On the evening of July 16, as negotiations were underway in Cesilienhoff Palace, an urgent coated cable arrived from Washington, babies satisfactorily born.

48
00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:13.480
[George] At 529 that morning in the desert of Alamogordo, New Mexico, the Trinity Test had detonated the world's first plutonium bomb, unleashing an incandescent blast equal to 21,000 tons of TNT that lit up the desert mountains and vaporized a 100-foot steel tower.

49
00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:18.440
[George] Armed with this terrifying knowledge, Truman found his confidence transformed.

50
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:28.360
[George] On July 24, Truman casually approached Stalin during a recess, mentioning that the United States possessed a new weapon of unusual destructive force.

51
00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:35.720
[George] Stalin smiled blandly, replied that he hoped the Americans would make good use of it against the Japanese, and walked away.

52
00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:43.800
[George] Unknown to Truman, Soviet atomic spy Klaus Futz had already provided detailed technical blueprints of the bomb to Moscow.

53
00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:47.960
[George] Stalin immediately cabled his scientists to redouble their efforts.

54
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:58.600
[George] The blinding detonation at Alamogordo signaled the arrival of an ominous new era where humanity possessed the apocalyptic capacity to annihilate its own civilization in an instant.

55
00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:07.560
[George] While Nazi Germany had surrendered on May 8, 1945, the Empire of Japan showed no willingness to surrender unconditionally.

56
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:15.760
[George] The brutal battles of Iwo Jima and Okinawa had revealed the terrifying fanaticism of Japanese resistance, on Okinawa alone.

57
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:27.520
[George] Over 12,000 Americans were killed, while more than 110,000 Japanese soldiers and 70,000 civilians died, many leaping off cliffs rather than surrender.

58
00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:46.160
[George] American intelligence intercepted Japanese operational plans for Ketsu Go, the defense of the home islands, mobilizing millions of soldiers, 10,000 kamikaze aircraft, and a civilian militia armed with bamboo spears under the slogan 100 million die together for the Emperor.

59
00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:57.920
[George] Joint Chiefs of Staff estimates for Operation Downfall, the planned two-stage amphibious invasion of Kyuju and Honshu set for November 1945 and March 1946.

60
00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:03.280
[George] Projected hundreds of thousands of Allied casualties and millions of Japanese deaths.

61
00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:19.600
[George] When the Potsdam Declaration of July 26 demanded Japan's immediate unconditional surrender or face, prompt and utter destruction, Tokyo's Prime Minister publicly announced that the government would Mokusatsu the ultimatum, killing it with contemptuous silence.

62
00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.920
[George] Truman hesitated no longer.

63
00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:32.800
[George] Faced with intelligence reports of millions of Japanese civilians prepared to sacrifice their lives in defense of the mainland, Truman sought the fastest possible termination of the slaughter.

64
00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:47.440
[George] At 8:15 on the morning of August 6, 1945, a lone American B-29 Superfortress named the Anola Gay, piloted by Colonel Paul Tibbets, flew over the Japanese industrial city of Hiroshima.

65
00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:54.640
[George] From 31,000 feet, the bombardier released Little Boy, a 9,000-pound uranium bomb.

66
00:08:54.640 --> 00:09:02.600
[George] Forty-three seconds later, at 1900 feet above the Shima Surgical Clinic, the weapon detonated in a blinding purple flash.

67
00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:18.280
[George] A fireball hotter than the surface of the sun vaporized 70,000 human beings in a fraction of a second, collapsing five square miles of the city into incandescent ash, followed by a towering mushroom cloud of black smoke and radioactive rain.

68
00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:22.760
[George] Still, the Japanese military council refused to surrender.

69
00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:38.200
[George] Three days later, on August 9, a second bomb, the plutonium weapon Fat Man, was dropped on Nagasaki, killing another 40,000 people, while the Soviet Red Army invaded Japanese-occupied Manchuria with one million troops.

70
00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:49.720
[George] Convinced that continued defiance meant the total annihilation of the Japanese race, Emperor Hirohito intervened in an unprecedented imperial conference, deciding to bear the unbearable.

71
00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:59.400
[George] On August 15, Hirohito's recorded voice was broadcast over Japanese radio for the first time in history, announcing Japan's surrender.

72
00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:00.520
[George] World War I.

73
00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:13.880
[George] The horrific devastation of Hiroshima and Nagasaki forced the Japanese military oligarchy to accept the reality of unconditional defeat, finally ending the global nightmare of World War II.

74
00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:18.040
[George] Harry Truman never flinched from the decision to use the atomic bomb.

75
00:10:18.040 --> 00:10:24.280
[George] He maintained on his desk in the Oval Office a famous wooden sign reading, The Buck stops here.

76
00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:29.240
[George] He argued steadfastly throughout his life that the bomb had ended the war in days.

77
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:42.440
[George] Saved hundreds of thousands of young American soldiers who would have died storming Japanese beaches, and spared millions of Japanese citizens from prolonged starvation, conventional firebombing, and imperial suicide.

78
00:10:42.440 --> 00:10:48.080
[George] Yet Truman was acutely aware that a terrible new threshold had been crossed in human history.

79
00:10:48.080 --> 00:11:01.360
[George] As he cautioned in his radio broadcast to the nation following Hiroshima, we must constitute ourselves trustees of this new force to prevent its misuse and to turn it into the channels of service to all mankind.

80
00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:12.640
[George] A modest farm boy from Missouri had stepped into the shoes of a giant, made the most cataclysmic decision in the annals of warfare, and ushered humanity into the perilous dawn of the nuclear era.

81
00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:24.080
[George] Truman's enduring willingness to take personal moral responsibility for the atomic bomb remains a masterclass in presidential accountability in the face of unimaginable tragedy.

82
00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:25.120
[Rebecca] Harry S.

83
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:32.400
[Rebecca] Truman ended World War II and confronted the dawn of the atomic age with the unvarnished courage of a frontier Missourian.

84
00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.640
[Rebecca] Yet peace brought no tranquility.

85
00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:50.800
[Rebecca] The collapse of the wartime alliance with the Soviet Union sparked the Cold War, labor strikes paralyzed American industry, and Truman's political fortunes sank so low that every newspaper predicted his utter annihilation in the election of 1948.

86
00:11:50.800 --> 00:11:54.160
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the second half of the Truman Saga.

87
00:11:54.160 --> 00:12:03.440
[Rebecca] The whistle stop Train Miracle, the Chicago Tribune headline, Dewey Defeats Truman, the Marshall Plan, and the Brutal Crucible of the Korean War.

88
00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:07.680
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

